<h2>1. Stack Memory Internals — Call Frames & Hardware Registers</h2>
<div class="theory-box">
<strong>Core Concept:</strong> The call stack is managed by two critical CPU hardware registers: the <strong>Stack Pointer (ESP/RSP)</strong> — which tracks the current top of the stack — and the <strong>Base/Frame Pointer (EBP/RBP)</strong> — which anchors the start of the current function's frame for stable local variable access. The stack grows <strong>downward</strong> toward lower memory addresses on x86/x86-64.
</div>
<div class="flow-diagram">
Calling Convention Sequence:
1. Push arguments onto stack (right-to-left in cdecl)
2. Push return address (next instruction after the call)
3. Save caller's EBP/RBP
4. Set EBP = current RSP (anchor new frame)
5. Subtract from RSP to allocate space for local variables
--- function body executes ---
6. Copy return value to EAX/RAX register
7. Restore RSP from EBP (deallocate locals)
8. Pop saved EBP (restore caller's frame)
9. RET: pop return address → jump to it
</div>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
void profileStackGrowth(int depth) {
    int localVar = 0x42;
    printf("Depth [%d] | Local variable address: %p\n",
           depth, (void*)&localVar);
    // Stack grows DOWNWARD — each nested call has a LOWER address
    if (depth > 1) profileStackGrowth(depth - 1);
}
int main(void) {
    profileStackGrowth(4);  // Observe decreasing addresses!
    return 0;
}</code></pre>

<h2>2. Heap Internals & Allocator Mechanics</h2>
<div class="theory-box">
<strong>Core Concept:</strong> The heap is managed by the C runtime allocator (e.g., glibc's ptmalloc). Each <code>malloc()</code> call does NOT directly call the OS — instead, it manages a pool of memory obtained via <code>sbrk()</code> or <code>mmap()</code>. Each allocated block has a <strong>metadata header</strong> storing its size and whether it's free or in-use. Freed blocks go into <strong>free lists</strong> for reuse, organized by size into bins.
</div>
<pre><code>// Heap chunk anatomy (glibc ptmalloc):
// [prev_size | size+flags | ... payload ... | next chunk header]
//  8 bytes      8 bytes     n bytes requested

// The MINIMUM_MALLOC overhead:
// malloc(1)    might actually allocate 32+ bytes (header + alignment)
// malloc(100)  might allocate 112 bytes (100 + 8 header + 4 padding)

// Free list behavior:
void *a = malloc(32);
void *b = malloc(32);
free(a);    // a's chunk goes into the 32-byte free bin
void *c = malloc(32);  // c may REUSE a's old chunk (same address!)</code></pre>

<h2>3. Memory Alignment & Structure Padding</h2>
<div class="theory-box">
<strong>Core Concept:</strong> Modern CPUs read data most efficiently when values are stored at <strong>natural alignment boundaries</strong>: a 4-byte int should be at an address divisible by 4; an 8-byte double should be at an address divisible by 8. The compiler automatically inserts invisible <strong>padding bytes</strong> between struct members to enforce these rules — making structs larger than the sum of their members.
</div>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
struct Unoptimized {
    char   a;    // 1 byte  @ offset 0
                 // 3 bytes PADDING (compiler inserts!)
    int    b;    // 4 bytes @ offset 4
    char   c;    // 1 byte  @ offset 8
                 // 3 bytes PADDING (to align struct size to 4 bytes)
};               // Total: 12 bytes (not 6!)

struct Optimized {
    int    b;    // 4 bytes @ offset 0
    char   a;    // 1 byte  @ offset 4
    char   c;    // 1 byte  @ offset 5
                 // 2 bytes padding
};               // Total: 8 bytes (saved 4 bytes by reordering!)

int main(void) {
    printf("Unoptimized: %zu bytes\n", sizeof(struct Unoptimized));  // 12
    printf("Optimized  : %zu bytes\n", sizeof(struct Optimized));    // 8
    // RULE: Order struct members LARGEST to SMALLEST for minimum padding
    return 0;
}</code></pre>
<div class="info-box"><strong>Optimization Rule:</strong> Declare struct members in order from largest to smallest type size. This minimizes padding. For embedded systems where memory is scarce, use <code>#pragma pack(1)</code> or <code>__attribute__((packed))</code> to eliminate all padding — but be aware this may cause performance penalties on aligned hardware.</div>

<h2>4. CPU Cache Locality</h2>
<div class="theory-box">
<strong>Core Concept:</strong> Modern CPUs don't fetch individual bytes — they fetch <strong>cache lines</strong> (typically 64 bytes). If your next access is within the same cache line, it's a cache <em>hit</em> (near-zero cost). If not, the CPU must fetch a new line from L2/L3/RAM — a cache <em>miss</em> that can be 100-1000x slower. Sequential (row-major) memory access maximizes cache hits; random or column-major access causes frequent misses.
</div>
<pre><code>#define N 1000
int mat[N][N];

// CACHE-FRIENDLY: row-major — accesses mat[0][0], mat[0][1], mat[0][2]...
// All in the same or adjacent cache lines
long sumRowMajor(void) {
    long s = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
        for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)
            s += mat[i][j];    // Sequential — prefetcher loves this
    return s;
}

// CACHE-HOSTILE: column-major — accesses mat[0][0], mat[1][0], mat[2][0]...
// Each access jumps N*4 = 4000 bytes — nearly always a cache miss!
long sumColMajor(void) {
    long s = 0;
    for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
            s += mat[i][j];    // Strided — cache miss every access
    return s;
}
// rowMajor is typically 3-10x FASTER than colMajor due to cache behavior!</code></pre>

<h2>5. Memory Leak Detection — Valgrind & AddressSanitizer</h2>
<pre><code>// Compile with debug symbols (REQUIRED for meaningful output)
// gcc -g -O0 -o program program.c

// ──── Valgrind Memcheck ────
// valgrind --leak-check=full --show-leak-kinds=all \
//          --track-origins=yes ./program
//
// Example output for a 40-byte leak:
// 40 bytes in 1 blocks are definitely lost in loss record 1 of 1
//    at 0x4C2FB0F: malloc (vg_replace_malloc.c:299)
//    by 0x40060A: main (program.c:5)
//
// ──── AddressSanitizer (ASan) — 2x overhead, much faster than Valgrind ────
// gcc -g -fsanitize=address -fsanitize=undefined -o program program.c
// ./program
// ASan reports errors in real-time with line numbers

// ──── Common issues Valgrind/ASan detect ────
// 1. Definitely lost: allocated memory with no remaining pointers
// 2. Invalid read/write: buffer overflow or use-after-free
// 3. Uninitialized value: reading malloc'd but never-written memory
// 4. Double free: calling free() twice on same pointer</code></pre>

<h2>6. Undefined Behavior (UB) — The C Standard's Dark Zone</h2>
<div class="theory-box">
<strong>Core Concept:</strong> When your code exhibits <strong>undefined behavior</strong>, the C standard makes absolutely <em>no guarantee</em> about what happens. The compiler is free to: delete the code entirely, return any value, produce a program that appears to work correctly but corrupts data silently, or generate a crash. Modern optimizing compilers <em>actively exploit</em> UB assumptions to generate faster code — which can make UB bugs nearly impossible to debug.
</div>
<table class="comparison-table">
<tr><th>UB Category</th><th>Example</th><th>Why Dangerous</th></tr>
<tr><td>NULL dereference</td><td>int *p=NULL; *p=5;</td><td>Immediate SIGSEGV crash</td></tr>
<tr><td>Array out-of-bounds</td><td>arr[10] on int arr[5]</td><td>Corrupts adjacent memory silently</td></tr>
<tr><td>Use-after-free (UAF)</td><td>free(p); *p=1;</td><td>Corrupts heap metadata; security exploit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Double-free</td><td>free(p); free(p);</td><td>Heap corruption; remote code execution risk</td></tr>
<tr><td>Signed int overflow</td><td>INT_MAX + 1</td><td>Compiler may assume it never happens; optimizes incorrectly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Strict aliasing violation</td><td>Casting int* to float*</td><td>Compiler may cache values separately; reads stale data</td></tr>
</table>
