<h2>1. Complex Pointer Declarations — The Right-Left Rule</h2>
<div class="theory-box">
<strong>Core Concept:</strong> C's declaration syntax obeys strict operator precedence: array brackets <code>[]</code> and function parentheses <code>()</code> bind more tightly than the pointer star <code>*</code>. To parse any complex declaration, start at the <strong>variable name</strong>, then alternate: scan RIGHT for <code>[]</code> or <code>()</code>, then LEFT for <code>*</code>, unwinding outer parentheses as you go.
</div>
<pre><code>// Parse: int *arr[10];
// Start at 'arr' → RIGHT: [10] → array of 10
// → LEFT: * → pointers to
// → LEFT: int → int
// RESULT: arr is an array of 10 pointers to int

// Parse: int (*ptr)[10];
// Start at 'ptr' → LEFT: * → pointer to
// → Unwind parens → RIGHT: [10] → array of 10
// → LEFT: int → int
// RESULT: ptr is a pointer to an array of 10 ints

// Parse: int (*cfg[5])(char);
// Start at 'cfg' → RIGHT: [5] → array of 5
// → LEFT: * → pointers to
// → Unwind → RIGHT: (char) → functions taking char
// → LEFT: int → returning int
// RESULT: cfg is an array of 5 pointers to functions(char)->int

// Use cdecl.org to verify any complex declaration!</code></pre>

<h2>2. Function Pointers — Syntax & Core Mechanics</h2>
<div class="theory-box">
<strong>Core Concept:</strong> Every function in C is stored at a fixed address in the <code>.text</code> segment of memory. A <strong>function pointer</strong> stores that address, enabling you to call functions <em>dynamically at runtime</em> — just like a regular variable holds a data address. Declaration syntax: <code>return_type (*pointer_name)(param_types);</code>
</div>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
int sub(int a, int b) { return a - b; }
int mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }

int main(void) {
    // Declare function pointer (note parentheses around *op)
    int (*op)(int, int);

    op = add;              // Assign — no & needed (functions decay to pointers)
    printf("3 + 4 = %d\n", op(3, 4));   // 7

    op = mul;
    printf("3 * 4 = %d\n", op(3, 4));   // 12

    // typedef for cleaner syntax
    typedef int (*BinaryOp)(int, int);
    BinaryOp operations[] = {add, sub, mul};
    const char *names[]   = {"add", "sub", "mul"};

    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
        printf("%s(10, 3) = %d\n", names[i], operations[i](10, 3));
    // Output: add=13, sub=7, mul=30
    return 0;
}</code></pre>

<h2>3. Jump Tables — O(1) Constant-Time Dispatch</h2>
<div class="theory-box">
<strong>Core Concept:</strong> An <strong>if-else chain</strong> with N conditions is O(N) — it must scan through each condition. An array of function pointers (jump table) uses an integer index for O(1) direct lookup regardless of how many cases exist. This is how switch statements are often compiled internally, and it's the pattern used in embedded OS interrupt dispatch tables.
</div>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
typedef void (*CommandHandler)(void);

void cmdHelp(void)  { printf("Commands: help, run, stop, exit\n"); }
void cmdRun(void)   { printf("Execution started...\n"); }
void cmdStop(void)  { printf("Execution halted.\n"); }
void cmdExit(void)  { printf("Goodbye!\n"); }

int main(void) {
    CommandHandler dispatch[] = {cmdHelp, cmdRun, cmdStop, cmdExit};
    const char *names[]       = {"help", "run", "stop", "exit"};
    const int   count         = 4;

    int choice = 1;   // Simulate user choosing "run"

    if (choice >= 0 && choice < count) {
        printf("Executing: %s\n", names[choice]);
        dispatch[choice]();   // O(1) — direct array index!
    }
    // vs if-else chain: O(4) — scans up to 4 conditions
    return 0;
}</code></pre>

<h2>4. Callbacks — Decoupled Algorithms</h2>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

// qsort's comparator: takes const void* for genericity
int compareAsc(const void *a, const void *b) {
    return (*(int*)a - *(int*)b);  // Cast void* → int*, dereference, subtract
}
int compareDesc(const void *a, const void *b) {
    return (*(int*)b - *(int*)a);
}

// Custom higher-order function: apply transform to each element
void mapArray(int *arr, int n, int (*transform)(int)) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = transform(arr[i]);
}
int doubleIt(int x) { return x * 2; }
int squareIt(int x) { return x * x; }

int main(void) {
    int data[] = {5, 2, 8, 1, 9, 3};
    int n = 6;

    qsort(data, n, sizeof(int), compareAsc);
    printf("Sorted: "); for (int i=0;i<n;i++) printf("%d ",data[i]);

    mapArray(data, n, squareIt);
    printf("\nSquared: "); for (int i=0;i<n;i++) printf("%d ",data[i]);
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}</code></pre>

<h2>5. void* Generic Programming</h2>
<pre><code>#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

// Generic swap — works on ANY data type using size parameter
void genericSwap(void *a, void *b, size_t size) {
    char temp[size];   // VLA as temporary buffer (C99)
    memcpy(temp, a,    size);
    memcpy(a,    b,    size);
    memcpy(b,    temp, size);
}

int main(void) {
    int x = 5, y = 10;
    genericSwap(&x, &y, sizeof(int));
    printf("x=%d y=%d\n", x, y);   // x=10 y=5

    double p = 3.14, q = 2.71;
    genericSwap(&p, &q, sizeof(double));
    printf("p=%.2f q=%.2f\n", p, q);   // p=2.71 q=3.14
    return 0;
}</code></pre>

<h2>6. Finite State Machines with Function Pointers</h2>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
typedef enum { IDLE, RUNNING, PAUSED, STOPPED } State;
typedef State (*StateHandler)(void);

State doIdle(void)    { printf("[IDLE] Waiting...\n");     return RUNNING;  }
State doRunning(void) { printf("[RUNNING] Processing...\n"); return PAUSED;   }
State doPaused(void)  { printf("[PAUSED] On hold.\n");     return STOPPED;  }
State doStopped(void) { printf("[STOPPED] Done.\n");       return STOPPED;  }

StateHandler stateTable[] = {doIdle, doRunning, doPaused, doStopped};

int main(void) {
    State current = IDLE;
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        current = stateTable[current]();  // O(1) state dispatch!
    return 0;
}</code></pre>
