<h2>1. GDB — The GNU Debugger</h2>
<div class="theory-box">
<strong>Core Concept:</strong> GDB attaches to a running process and sets <strong>hardware breakpoints</strong> — trap instructions at designated source lines. When the processor hits a trap, it pauses execution, letting you inspect stack registers, query variable values in real-time, and trace back the function call history. Always compile with <code>-g -O0</code> for debugging: <code>-g</code> embeds source line information; <code>-O0</code> disables optimizations that would reorder or eliminate code you're trying to inspect.
</div>
<table class="comparison-table">
<tr><th>GDB Command</th><th>Action</th></tr>
<tr><td>break main / break file.c:42</td><td>Set breakpoint at function or line</td></tr>
<tr><td>run [args]</td><td>Start program execution</td></tr>
<tr><td>next (n)</td><td>Execute one line; step OVER function calls</td></tr>
<tr><td>step (s)</td><td>Execute one line; step INTO function calls</td></tr>
<tr><td>finish</td><td>Run until current function returns</td></tr>
<tr><td>print varName</td><td>Print current value of variable</td></tr>
<tr><td>print *ptr</td><td>Dereference and print pointer target</td></tr>
<tr><td>watch varName</td><td>Break whenever variable value changes</td></tr>
<tr><td>backtrace (bt)</td><td>Print complete call stack to current point</td></tr>
<tr><td>frame N</td><td>Switch to stack frame N in backtrace</td></tr>
<tr><td>info locals</td><td>Show all local variables in current frame</td></tr>
<tr><td>x/10xw 0xADDR</td><td>Examine 10 hex words at memory address</td></tr>
<tr><td>continue (c)</td><td>Resume until next breakpoint</td></tr>
<tr><td>quit (q)</td><td>Exit GDB</td></tr>
</table>
<pre><code>// Typical debugging workflow:
// 1. Compile: gcc -g -O0 -o program program.c
// 2. Start:   gdb ./program
// (gdb) break main
// (gdb) run
// (gdb) next         ← step through code
// (gdb) print ptr    ← check pointer value
// (gdb) print *ptr   ← dereference pointer
// (gdb) backtrace    ← where am I in the call stack?
// (gdb) info locals  ← what are all local variable values?
// (gdb) quit</code></pre>

<h2>2. Code Optimization Techniques</h2>
<div class="theory-box">
<strong>Optimization Hierarchy:</strong> Always optimize in this order: (1) Algorithm complexity O(N) → (2) Memory cache alignment → (3) Compiler flags (-O2/-O3) → (4) Manual low-level tricks. Fixing an O(n²) algorithm to O(n log n) will outperform any bitwise tricks on the same O(n²) algorithm.
</div>
<pre><code>// Technique 1: Loop unrolling — reduce iteration overhead
// Before:
for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) arr[i] = 0;
// After (what -O2 does automatically):
arr[0]=arr[1]=arr[2]=arr[3]=arr[4]=arr[5]=arr[6]=arr[7]=0;

// Technique 2: Strength reduction
int x = n * 8;   // Multiply
int y = n << 3;  // Equivalent left shift — may be faster

// Technique 3: Hoist loop-invariant computations
double radius = 5.0;
// SLOW: recomputes sqrt(2*PI) every iteration
for (int i=0; i<10000; i++) area[i] = radius * sqrt(2.0 * 3.14159);
// FAST: compute once outside the loop
double factor = radius * sqrt(2.0 * 3.14159);
for (int i=0; i<10000; i++) area[i] = factor;

// Technique 4: Prefer local variables over globals in hot loops
// (globals require memory access; locals may be in registers)
int local_count = globalCounter;
for (int i=0; i<1000000; i++) local_count++;
globalCounter = local_count;</code></pre>

<h2>3. Secure Coding — SEI CERT C Standards</h2>
<pre><code>#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;limits.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;

// ✗ NEVER USE these insecure functions:
gets(buf);                          // No bounds check → CVE generator
strcpy(dst, src);                   // No bounds check → stack smash
sprintf(buf, user_format, data);    // Format string attack!

// ✓ ALWAYS USE these safe alternatives:
fgets(buf, sizeof(buf), stdin);
strncpy(dst, src, sizeof(dst)-1); dst[sizeof(dst)-1] = '\0';
snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "%s", user_data);   // NEVER pass user data as format!

// Integer overflow check (SEI CERT INT30-C)
int safeAdd(int a, int b, int *result) {
    if ((b > 0 && a > INT_MAX - b) ||
        (b < 0 && a < INT_MIN - b)) {
        return 0;  // Would overflow
    }
    *result = a + b;
    return 1;
}

// Validate all pointer parameters before use
void processData(const char *data, size_t len) {
    if (!data)          return;   // NULL check
    if (len == 0)       return;   // Empty check
    if (len > MAX_LEN)  return;   // Size bounds check
    // Now safe to use data...
}</code></pre>

<h2>4. POSIX Multithreading with pthreads</h2>
<div class="theory-box">
<strong>Core Concept:</strong> A <strong>race condition</strong> occurs when two or more threads access shared data concurrently and the outcome depends on which thread runs first. Race conditions produce non-deterministic, intermittent bugs that are nearly impossible to reproduce reliably. The solution is a <strong>mutex</strong> (mutual exclusion lock) — only one thread can hold the mutex at a time; others block until it's released.
</div>
<pre><code>#include &lt;pthread.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
int sharedCounter = 0;

void* workerThread(void *arg) {
    int id = *(int*)arg;
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);    // ACQUIRE — blocks if another thread holds it
        sharedCounter++;              // Critical section — only ONE thread at a time!
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);  // RELEASE — lets next thread in
    }
    printf("Thread %d done.\n", id);
    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t threads[4];
    int ids[4] = {0, 1, 2, 3};

    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, workerThread, &ids[i]);

    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);   // Wait for each thread to finish

    printf("Final counter: %d\n", sharedCounter);  // Must be exactly 4000
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&lock);
    return 0;
}
// Compile: gcc -pthread -o program program.c</code></pre>

<h2>5. Network Socket Programming</h2>
<pre><code>#include &lt;sys/socket.h&gt;
#include &lt;netinet/in.h&gt;
#include &lt;arpa/inet.h&gt;
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;

// ── TCP Server ──
int serverFd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);  // Create TCP socket

// Allow address reuse (avoid "Address already in use" on restart)
int opt = 1;
setsockopt(serverFd, SOL_SOCKET, SO_REUSEADDR, &opt, sizeof(opt));

struct sockaddr_in addr = {
    .sin_family      = AF_INET,
    .sin_port        = htons(8080),    // htons: host→network byte order
    .sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY      // Accept on all network interfaces
};

bind(serverFd, (struct sockaddr*)&addr, sizeof(addr));
listen(serverFd, 5);   // Queue up to 5 pending connections

int clientFd = accept(serverFd, NULL, NULL);   // Block until client connects

char buf[1024] = {0};
recv(clientFd, buf, sizeof(buf)-1, 0);         // Receive data
printf("Received: %s\n", buf);

const char *response = "HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nContent-Length: 5\r\n\r\nHello";
send(clientFd, response, strlen(response), 0); // Send response

close(clientFd);
close(serverFd);</code></pre>

