{"body":"<p>Modern C++ (C++11 and later) encourages the use of <strong>Smart Pointers</strong> to automate memory management via RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization).</p>
<h2>unique_ptr</h2>
<p>A <code>unique_ptr</code> owns the object exclusively. When it goes out of scope, the memory is automatically deleted.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;memory&gt;

class Entity {
public:
    Entity() { cout &lt;&lt; "Created
"; }
    ~Entity() { cout &lt;&lt; "Destroyed
"; }
    void print() { cout &lt;&lt; "Printing
"; }
};

int main() {
    {
        // Preferred way to create a unique_ptr (C++14)
        std::unique_ptr&lt;Entity&gt; entity = std::make_unique&lt;Entity&gt;();
        entity-&gt;print();
    } // "Destroyed" is automatically printed here! No delete needed!
    return 0;
}</code></pre>
<h2>shared_ptr</h2>
<p>Use <code>shared_ptr</code> when multiple owners need to point to the same memory. It keeps a reference count and deletes the memory when the count reaches zero.</p>
