{"body":"<p><strong>RAII</strong> (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) is the core idiom of C++. It dictates that resource management (memory, file handles, network sockets) should be tied to object lifespan.</p>
<h2>The Rule of Three</h2>
<p>If your class manually manages memory (e.g., holds a raw pointer and uses <code>delete</code> in the destructor), you MUST implement three things to avoid shallow copy bugs:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Destructor:</strong> To free the memory.</li>
  <li><strong>Copy Constructor:</strong> To create a deep copy of the memory.</li>
  <li><strong>Copy Assignment Operator:</strong> To handle assignment of existing objects safely.</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>class StringClass {
private:
    char* buffer;
public:
    // 1. Destructor
    ~StringClass() { delete[] buffer; }

    // 2. Copy Constructor
    StringClass(const StringClass& other) {
        // Allocate new memory and copy data
    }

    // 3. Copy Assignment Operator
    StringClass& operator=(const StringClass& other) {
        if (this != &other) {
            // Clean up old memory, allocate new, and copy
        }
        return *this;
    }
};</code></pre>
<blockquote>With modern C++ and Smart Pointers, we often aim for the <strong>Rule of Zero</strong>: design classes so they don't require any manual resource management at all!</blockquote>"}

{"body":"<p>Writing R code that works correctly on clean data is only half the job. Real-world data is messy, files go missing, and APIs time out. <strong>Robust R scripts</strong> anticipate failure and handle it gracefully instead of crashing mid-pipeline.</p>
<h2>The tryCatch() Function</h2>
<p><code>tryCatch()</code> is R's primary mechanism for catching errors and warnings. It evaluates an expression and, if it throws an error or warning, executes a handler instead of stopping execution.</p>
<pre><code># Basic tryCatch structure
result <- tryCatch({
  log(-1)          # Produces NaN with a warning
  sqrt("hello")    # Produces an error
}, warning = function(w) {
  message("Warning caught: ", conditionMessage(w))
  return(NA)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error caught: ", conditionMessage(e))
  return(NULL)
}, finally = {
  message("This always runs, error or not.")
})

print(result)  # NULL (error was caught)</code></pre>
<h2>Signalling Conditions</h2>
<p>You can create your own errors, warnings, and messages using three built-in functions:</p>
<pre><code>validate_age <- function(age) {
  if (!is.numeric(age)) stop("age must be numeric")
  if (age < 0)          stop("age cannot be negative")
  if (age > 120)        warning("age seems unusually high")
  message("Age validated: ", age)
  return(age)
}

tryCatch(validate_age("twenty"),
         error = function(e) cat("Caught:", e$message))</code></pre>
<h2>withCallingHandlers — Non-Exiting Handlers</h2>
<p>Unlike <code>tryCatch()</code>, <code>withCallingHandlers()</code> allows execution to resume after handling a condition, making it ideal for logging warnings without stopping the pipeline.</p>
<pre><code>withCallingHandlers({
  log(-1)       # warning
  log(10)       # continues normally after handler runs
}, warning = function(w) {
  cat("LOG:", conditionMessage(w), "
")
  invokeRestart("muffleWarning")   # suppress the warning
})</code></pre>
<h2>Debugging Tools in RStudio</h2>
<pre><code># 1. traceback() — shows the call stack after an error
f <- function() g()
g <- function() stop("boom")
f()
traceback()

# 2. browser() — pauses execution and opens interactive debugger
my_func <- function(x) {
  browser()   # Execution stops here; type ls() to see variables
  x * 2
}

# 3. debug() — same as inserting browser() at the start of a function
debug(my_func)
my_func(5)
undebug(my_func)

# 4. debugonce() — debug only the next call
debugonce(my_func)</code></pre>
<h2>Safe Wrappers with purrr</h2>
<p>The <code>purrr</code> package provides <code>safely()</code> and <code>possibly()</code> to create error-safe versions of any function, perfect for iterating over data that may partially fail.</p>
<pre><code>library(purrr)

safe_log <- safely(log)
safe_log(10)    # list(result = 2.302585, error = NULL)
safe_log("x")  # list(result = NULL, error = <error>)

# possibly() returns a default value on error
possible_log <- possibly(log, otherwise = NA_real_)
map_dbl(list(10, -1, "x", 100), possible_log)
# 2.302585  NaN       NA  4.605170</code></pre>"}

{"body":"<p>JavaScript is <strong>single-threaded</strong> — it can only do one thing at a time. But thanks to the <strong>Event Loop</strong>, it can handle slow operations (network requests, timers) without freezing the UI. Understanding async programming is what separates junior developers from seniors.</p>

<h2>The Event Loop: How JS Really Works</h2>
<p>JavaScript has three parts working together:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Call Stack:</strong> Where code executes. One frame at a time.</li>
  <li><strong>Web APIs:</strong> Browser features (setTimeout, fetch, DOM events) that run outside the stack.</li>
  <li><strong>Task Queue (Macrotask):</strong> Callbacks waiting to execute after the stack is clear.</li>
  <li><strong>Microtask Queue:</strong> Promise callbacks. These run BEFORE macrotasks.</li>
</ul>

<pre><code>console.log("1");
setTimeout(() => console.log("2"), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("3"));
console.log("4");

// Output: 1, 4, 3, 2
// Why? Synchronous code (1, 4) runs first.
// Then microtasks (Promise .then) run.
// Then macrotasks (setTimeout) run.</code></pre>

<h2>Callbacks: The Old Way</h2>
<pre><code>function fetchData(callback) {
  setTimeout(() => {
    callback("Data loaded!");
  }, 1000);
}

fetchData((result) => {
  console.log(result); // "Data loaded!"
});

// Callback hell (nested callbacks)
getUser(user => {
  getOrders(user.id, orders => {
    getProducts(orders[0].id, products => {
      console.log(products); // Deep nesting = hard to read
    });
  });
});</code></pre>

<h2>Promises: A Better Contract</h2>
<p>A <strong>Promise</strong> is an object representing the eventual completion (or failure) of an async operation.</p>
<pre><code>const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  setTimeout(() => {
    const success = true;
    if (success) {
      resolve("Operation succeeded!");
    } else {
      reject("Operation failed!");
    }
  }, 1000);
});

promise
  .then(result => console.log(result))   // "Operation succeeded!"
  .catch(error => console.error(error))
  .finally(() => console.log("Done"));</code></pre>

<h2>Promise Chaining</h2>
<pre><code>fetch('https://api.github.com/users/octocat')
  .then(response => {
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
    return response.json(); // returns a new Promise
  })
  .then(data => {
    console.log(data.name); // "The Octocat"
    return data.repos_url;
  })
  .then(reposUrl => fetch(reposUrl))
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(repos => console.log(repos.length))
  .catch(err => console.error("Fetch failed:", err));</code></pre>

<h2>Promise.all & Promise.race</h2>
<pre><code>// Promise.all — wait for ALL to succeed (fail fast)
const urls = ['url1', 'url2', 'url3'];
Promise.all(urls.map(url => fetch(url)))
  .then(responses => console.log("All loaded!"))
  .catch(err => console.error("One failed:", err));

// Promise.allSettled — wait for ALL to finish (never rejects)
Promise.allSettled([promise1, promise2])
  .then(results => {
    results.forEach(r => {
      if (r.status === 'fulfilled') console.log(r.value);
      else console.error(r.reason);
    });
  });

// Promise.race — first to settle wins
Promise.race([fetch(url), timeout(5000)])
  .then(result => console.log("Won the race!"));</code></pre>

<h2>async / await: Syntactic Sugar</h2>
<p>Makes async code look synchronous. Easier to read, easier to debug.</p>
<pre><code>async function getGitHubUser(username) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`https://api.github.com/users/${username}`);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);

    const data = await response.json();
    console.log(data.name);
    return data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Failed to fetch user:", error);
    throw error; // Re-throw if caller needs to handle it
  }
}

// async functions ALWAYS return a Promise
getGitHubUser("octocat").then(user => console.log(user));

// Parallel fetching with await
async function getUsers(usernames) {
  const promises = usernames.map(u => getGitHubUser(u));
  const users = await Promise.all(promises);
  return users;
}</code></pre>

<h2>AbortController: Cancel Requests</h2>
<pre><code>const controller = new AbortController();

fetch('/api/data', { signal: controller.signal })
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data))
  .catch(err => {
    if (err.name === 'AbortError') {
      console.log('Request was cancelled');
    }
  });

// Cancel after 5 seconds
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000);</code></pre>"}

{"body":"<p>Functions in JavaScript are <strong>first-class citizens</strong>. They can be passed around like any other value. This enables incredibly powerful patterns: closures that remember their birthplace, functions that create functions, and generators that pause and resume execution.</p>

<h2>Closures: The Heart of JavaScript</h2>
<p>A <strong>closure</strong> is a function that remembers the variables from its outer scope <em>even after the outer function has finished executing</em>.</p>
<pre><code>function createCounter() {
  let count = 0; // "Private" variable

  return {
    increment() { return ++count; },
    decrement() { return --count; },
    getCount() { return count; }
  };
}

const counter = createCounter();
console.log(counter.increment()); // 1
console.log(counter.increment()); // 2
console.log(counter.getCount());  // 2
console.log(counter.count);       // undefined — truly private!</code></pre>

<blockquote><strong>Why closures matter:</strong> They enable <em>data privacy</em> (before classes had private fields), <em>factory functions</em>, and <em>partial application</em>. Every callback in an event listener is a closure.</blockquote>

<h2>Higher-Order Functions</h2>
<p>A function that takes a function as an argument, or returns a function.</p>
<pre><code>// Function that returns a function
function multiplyBy(factor) {
  return function(number) {
    return number * factor;
  };
}

const double = multiplyBy(2);
const triple = multiplyBy(3);

console.log(double(5)); // 10
console.log(triple(5)); // 15

// Function that takes a function (like Array.prototype.map)
function withLogging(fn) {
  return function(...args) {
    console.log(`Calling ${fn.name} with `, args);
    const result = fn(...args);
    console.log(`Result: ${result}`);
    return result;
  };
}

const loggedAdd = withLogging((a, b) => a + b);
loggedAdd(2, 3);
// Calling anonymous with [2, 3]
// Result: 5</code></pre>

<h2>Currying</h2>
<p>Transform a function with multiple arguments into a sequence of functions, each taking a single argument.</p>
<pre><code>// Normal function
const add = (a, b, c) => a + b + c;
add(1, 2, 3); // 6

// Curried version
const addCurried = a => b => c => a + b + c;
addCurried(1)(2)(3); // 6

// Practical use: creating specialized functions
const addTax = rate => price => price * (1 + rate);
const addVAT = addTax(0.2);  // 20% VAT
const addSalesTax = addTax(0.08); // 8% sales tax

console.log(addVAT(100));      // 120
console.log(addSalesTax(100)); // 108

// Generic curry helper
function curry(fn) {
  return function curried(...args) {
    if (args.length >= fn.length) {
      return fn.apply(this, args);
    }
    return function(...nextArgs) {
      return curried.apply(this, args.concat(nextArgs));
    };
  };
}</code></pre>

<h2>Memoization</h2>
<p>Cache function results to avoid redundant computation. Essential for performance in recursive algorithms.</p>
<pre><code>function memoize(fn) {
  const cache = new Map();
  return function(...args) {
    const key = JSON.stringify(args);
    if (cache.has(key)) {
      console.log("Cache hit!");
      return cache.get(key);
    }
    const result = fn.apply(this, args);
    cache.set(key, result);
    return result;
  };
}

// Fibonacci with memoization
const fib = memoize(function(n) {
  if (n &lt;= 1) return n;
  return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
});

console.log(fib(40)); // Fast! Without memoization, this would hang.</code></pre>

<h2>IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression)</h2>
<p>Create a private scope without polluting the global namespace.</p>
<pre><code>(function() {
  const secret = "I am private";
  console.log(secret);
})();

// Modern alternative: just use a block
{
  const secret = "I am also private";
  console.log(secret);
}

// IIFE with arrow function
(() => {
  console.log("Running immediately!");
})();</code></pre>

<h2>Generators (function*)</h2>
<p>Functions that can <strong>pause</strong> execution and <strong>resume</strong> later. Perfect for infinite sequences, lazy evaluation, and async iteration.</p>
<pre><code>function* idGenerator() {
  let id = 1;
  while (true) {
    yield id++;
  }
}

const gen = idGenerator();
console.log(gen.next().value); // 1
console.log(gen.next().value); // 2
console.log(gen.next().value); // 3

// Generator for pagination
function* paginate(items, pageSize) {
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; items.length; i += pageSize) {
    yield items.slice(i, i + pageSize);
  }
}

const pages = paginate([1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8], 3);
for (const page of pages) {
  console.log(page); // [1,2,3], [4,5,6], [7,8]
}

// yield* for delegation
function* combined() {
  yield* [1, 2, 3];
  yield* ["a", "b", "c"];
}
// 1, 2, 3, "a", "b", "c"</code></pre>"}

{"body":"<p>JavaScript is often called an "object-oriented" language, but it is more accurately <strong>prototype-based</strong>. Objects inherit directly from other objects. ES6 <code>class</code> syntax is just syntactic sugar over this prototype system.</p>

<h2>The this Keyword</h2>
<p>The value of <code>this</code> depends on <strong>how a function is called</strong>, not where it is defined. This is the #1 source of confusion in JavaScript.</p>
<pre><code>const user = {
  name: "Alice",
  greet() {
    console.log(`Hello, I am ${this.name}`);
  }
};

user.greet(); // "Hello, I am Alice" — this = user

const greetFn = user.greet;
greetFn(); // "Hello, I am undefined" — this = global/window (strict: undefined)

// Fixing with bind
const boundGreet = user.greet.bind(user);
boundGreet(); // "Hello, I am Alice"

// Arrow functions inherit 'this' from outer scope
const team = {
  name: "DevSquad",
  members: ["Alice", "Bob"],
  showMembers() {
    this.members.forEach(member => {
      console.log(`${this.name}: ${member}`);
      // 'this' is team, because arrow function inherits it
    });
  }
};</code></pre>

<h2>The 4 Rules of this</h2>
<pre><code>// 1. Default binding (standalone function call)
function showThis() { console.log(this); }
showThis(); // window (non-strict) or undefined (strict)

// 2. Implicit binding (method call)
obj.method(); // this = obj

// 3. Explicit binding (call, apply, bind)
showThis.call(obj);   // this = obj
showThis.apply(obj);  // this = obj
const bound = showThis.bind(obj); // this = obj (permanent)

// 4. new binding
function Person(name) {
  this.name = name; // this = the new object
}
const p = new Person("Alice");</code></pre>

<h2>Prototypes: The Real Inheritance</h2>
<p>Every JavaScript object has an internal link to another object called its <strong>prototype</strong>. When you access a property, JS looks up the prototype chain.</p>
<pre><code>const animal = {
  eat() {
    console.log(`${this.name} is eating.`);
  }
};

const dog = {
  name: "Rex",
  bark() {
    console.log("Woof!");
  }
};

// Set dog's prototype to animal
Object.setPrototypeOf(dog, animal);
// dog.__proto__ = animal (deprecated but works)

dog.bark(); // "Woof!" (found on dog)
dog.eat();  // "Rex is eating." (found on animal via prototype chain)

// Check prototype
console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(dog) === animal); // true</code></pre>

<h2>Constructor Functions</h2>
<pre><code>function User(name, email) {
  this.name = name;
  this.email = email;
}

// Methods go on the prototype (shared, not duplicated per instance)
User.prototype.greet = function() {
  return `Hello, I am ${this.name}`;
};

const user1 = new User("Alice", "alice@example.com");
const user2 = new User("Bob", "bob@example.com");

console.log(user1.greet()); // "Hello, I am Alice"
console.log(user1.greet === user2.greet); // true (same function!)</code></pre>

<h2>ES6 Classes: Syntactic Sugar</h2>
<pre><code>class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  speak() {
    console.log(`${this.name} makes a sound.`);
  }

  // Static method — called on the class, not instances
  static isAnimal(obj) {
    return obj instanceof Animal;
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  constructor(name, breed) {
    super(name); // Call parent constructor
    this.breed = breed;
  }

  speak() {
    console.log(`${this.name} barks!`);
  }

  fetch() {
    console.log(`${this.name} is fetching.`);
  }
}

const rex = new Dog("Rex", "German Shepherd");
rex.speak();  // "Rex barks!" (overridden method)
rex.fetch();  // "Rex is fetching."
console.log(Dog.isAnimal(rex)); // true</code></pre>

<h2>Private Fields (ES2022)</h2>
<pre><code>class BankAccount {
  #balance = 0; // Private field — truly inaccessible from outside

  constructor(initialBalance) {
    this.#balance = initialBalance;
  }

  deposit(amount) {
    if (amount > 0) this.#balance += amount;
  }

  getBalance() {
    return this.#balance;
  }
}

const account = new BankAccount(100);
account.deposit(50);
console.log(account.getBalance()); // 150
// console.log(account.#balance);  // ❌ SyntaxError! Truly private.</code></pre>

<h2>Composition over Inheritance</h2>
<p>Inheritance creates tight coupling. Composition is more flexible.</p>
<pre><code>// Instead of class Dog extends Animal...
const canSpeak = (state) => ({
  speak: () => console.log(`${state.name} says hello!`)
});

const canFly = (state) => ({
  fly: () => console.log(`${state.name} is flying!`)
});

const createBird = (name) => {
  const state = { name };
  return {
    ...canSpeak(state),
    ...canFly(state)
  };
};

const parrot = createBird("Polly");
parrot.speak(); // "Polly says hello!"
parrot.fly();   // "Polly is flying!"</code></pre>"}

{"body":"<p>Professional developers do not write bug-free code — they write code that <strong>fails gracefully</strong> and is <strong>easy to debug</strong>. Mastering error handling and debugging tools will save you hours of frustration.</p>

<h2>try / catch / finally</h2>
<p>Wrap risky code in <code>try</code>. If an error occurs, <code>catch</code> handles it. <code>finally</code> always runs — perfect for cleanup.</p>
<pre><code>function parseJSON(jsonString) {
  try {
    const data = JSON.parse(jsonString);
    return data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Invalid JSON:", error.message);
    return null; // Graceful fallback
  } finally {
    console.log("Parsing attempt complete.");
  }
}

parseJSON('{"name": "Alice"}'); // ✅ Returns object
parseJSON("not json");            // ❌ Logs error, returns null</code></pre>

<h2>throw: Creating Errors</h2>
<pre><code>function divide(a, b) {
  if (typeof a !== 'number' || typeof b !== 'number') {
    throw new TypeError("Both arguments must be numbers");
  }
  if (b === 0) {
    throw new Error("Cannot divide by zero");
  }
  return a / b;
}

try {
  divide(10, 0);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.name);    // "Error"
  console.log(err.message); // "Cannot divide by zero"
}</code></pre>

<h2>Custom Error Classes</h2>
<pre><code>class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(field, message) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "ValidationError";
    this.field = field;
  }
}

class NetworkError extends Error {
  constructor(statusCode, message) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "NetworkError";
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
  }
}

function validateUser(user) {
  if (!user.email) {
    throw new ValidationError("email", "Email is required");
  }
  if (!user.email.includes("@")) {
    throw new ValidationError("email", "Invalid email format");
  }
}

try {
  validateUser({ name: "Alice" });
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof ValidationError) {
    console.log(`Field '${err.field}' failed: ${err.message}`);
  } else {
    console.error("Unexpected error:", err);
  }
}</code></pre>

<h2>Error Handling in Async Code</h2>
<pre><code>// With Promises
fetch('/api/user')
  .then(response => {
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new NetworkError(response.status, 'Failed to fetch user');
    }
    return response.json();
  })
  .catch(err => {
    if (err instanceof NetworkError) {
      console.error(`Network error ${err.statusCode}: ${err.message}`);
    }
  });

// With async/await
async function fetchUser() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('/api/user');
    if (!response.ok) throw new NetworkError(response.status, 'Failed');
    return await response.json();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Fetch failed:", err.message);
    return null; // Graceful fallback
  }
}</code></pre>

<h2>console Methods You Should Know</h2>
<pre><code>console.log("Basic log");
console.warn("Warning!");        // Yellow warning
console.error("Error!");         // Red error
console.info("Info message");

console.table([{name:"Alice",age:25},{name:"Bob",age:30}]);
// Displays as a beautiful table

console.group("User Details");
console.log("Name: Alice");
console.log("Role: Admin");
console.groupEnd();

console.time("loop");
for (let i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {}
console.timeEnd("loop"); // "loop: 2.345ms"

console.assert(1 === 2, "This will print because the assertion failed");

console.trace("Trace here"); // Prints the call stack</code></pre>

<h2>The debugger Statement</h2>
<pre><code>function calculateTotal(items) {
  let total = 0;
  for (const item of items) {
    debugger; // Execution PAUSES here if DevTools is open!
    total += item.price * item.quantity;
  }
  return total;
}

// When DevTools is open, the browser pauses at 'debugger'
// You can inspect variables, step through code, and watch expressions.</code></pre>

<h2>Reading Stack Traces</h2>
<pre><code>function a() { b(); }
function b() { c(); }
function c() { throw new Error("Something broke!"); }

try {
  a();
} catch (err) {
  console.error(err.stack);
  // Error: Something broke!
  //     at c (file.js:3)
  //     at b (file.js:2)
  //     at a (file.js:1)
  // Read from BOTTOM to TOP to trace the call path!
}</code></pre>"}

{"body":"<p>Modern JavaScript development is not just about writing code — it is about <strong>organizing</strong>, <strong>transforming</strong>, and <strong>optimizing</strong> it. Understanding modules and build tools is essential for any professional developer.</p>

<h2>ES Modules: The Standard</h2>
<p>Before ES6, JavaScript had no native module system. We used script tags (global pollution) or AMD/CommonJS. ES Modules changed everything.</p>
<pre><code>// utils.js — named exports
export const PI = 3.14159;
export function add(a, b) { return a + b; }

// math.js — default export
export default function calculateArea(radius) {
  return PI * radius * radius;
}

// main.js — importing
import calculateArea, { add, PI } from './math.js';
import * as math from './math.js';  // namespace import

// Dynamic import (lazy loading)
const module = await import('./heavy-module.js');
module.heavyFunction();</code></pre>

<h2>npm & package.json</h2>
<p><strong>npm</strong> (Node Package Manager) is the world's largest software registry. <code>package.json</code> defines your project's dependencies and scripts.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "name": "my-app",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "type": "module",  // Enable ES modules in Node.js
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "vite",
    "build": "vite build",
    "preview": "vite preview"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "react": "^18.2.0",
    "lodash": "^4.17.21"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "vite": "^5.0.0",
    "eslint": "^8.0.0"
  }
}

// Commands:
// npm install          — install all dependencies
// npm install lodash   — install a package
// npm run dev          — run the "dev" script
// npm update           — update packages</code></pre>

<h2>Webpack: The Classic Bundler</h2>
<p>Webpack takes your modules and bundles them into optimized files for the browser.</p>
<pre><code>// webpack.config.js
module.exports = {
  entry: './src/index.js',
  output: {
    filename: 'bundle.[contenthash].js',
    path: path.resolve(__dirname, 'dist')
  },
  module: {
    rules: [
      { test: /.js$/, use: 'babel-loader' },
      { test: /.css$/, use: ['style-loader', 'css-loader'] }
    ]
  },
  optimization: {
    splitChunks: { chunks: 'all' } // Code splitting
  }
};</code></pre>

<h2>Vite: The Modern Choice</h2>
<p>Vite uses native ES modules in development (no bundling needed!) and Rollup for production builds. It is <strong>significantly faster</strong> than Webpack.</p>
<pre><code>// vite.config.js
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import react from '@vitejs/plugin-react';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [react()],
  build: {
    outDir: 'dist',
    minify: 'terser',
    sourcemap: true
  }
});

// Start dev server: npm run dev (instant!)
// Production build: npm run build</code></pre>

<h2>Babel: Transpilation</h2>
<p>Babel converts modern JavaScript into code that older browsers can understand.</p>
<pre><code>// Input (ES6+)
const greet = (name) => `Hello, ${name}!`;
const user = { name, age };

// Output (ES5 for old browsers)
var greet = function greet(name) {
  return "Hello, " + name + "!";
};
var user = { name: name, age: age };

// .babelrc
{
  "presets": ["@babel/preset-env"]
}</code></pre>

<h2>Tree Shaking</h2>
<p>Modern bundlers can <strong>eliminate dead code</strong> — removing exports that are never imported.</p>
<pre><code>// utils.js
export function used() { return "I am used"; }
export function unused() { return "I am dead code"; }

// main.js
import { used } from './utils.js';
console.log(used());

// With tree shaking, 'unused' is NOT included in the final bundle!
// This keeps your app small and fast.</code></pre>"}

{"body":"<p>Writing code is only half the battle. Professional developers ensure their code is <strong>tested</strong>, <strong>fast</strong>, and <strong>memory-efficient</strong>. This module covers the practices that separate hobby projects from production systems.</p>

<h2>Unit Testing with Jest</h2>
<p><strong>Jest</strong> is the most popular JavaScript testing framework. It provides test runners, assertions, mocking, and coverage reports out of the box.</p>
<pre><code>// math.js
export function add(a, b) { return a + b; }
export function divide(a, b) {
  if (b === 0) throw new Error("Cannot divide by zero");
  return a / b;
}

// math.test.js
import { add, divide } from './math.js';

describe('Math utilities', () => {
  test('adds two numbers', () => {
    expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
    expect(add(-1, 1)).toBe(0);
  });

  test('divides two numbers', () => {
    expect(divide(10, 2)).toBe(5);
  });

  test('throws on division by zero', () => {
    expect(() => divide(10, 0)).toThrow("Cannot divide by zero");
  });
});

// Run: npx jest
// Coverage: npx jest --coverage</code></pre>

<h2>Mocking & Spies</h2>
<pre><code>import { fetchUser } from './api.js';
import { getUserProfile } from './user.js';

// Mock the module
jest.mock('./api.js');

test('fetches user profile', async () => {
  fetchUser.mockResolvedValue({ id: 1, name: "Alice" });

  const profile = await getUserProfile(1);
  expect(profile.name).toBe("Alice");
  expect(fetchUser).toHaveBeenCalledWith(1);
  expect(fetchUser).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
});

// Spy on existing function
const spy = jest.spyOn(console, 'log');
myFunction();
expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalledWith("Expected output");
spy.mockRestore();</code></pre>

<h2>Performance Optimization</h2>
<pre><code>// 1. Avoid nested loops when possible
// O(n²) → O(n) using a Set or Map

// 2. Use requestAnimationFrame for animations
function animate() {
  // Update DOM
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
}

// 3. Debounce expensive operations
function debounce(fn, delay) {
  let timer;
  return function(...args) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), delay);
  };
}

const search = debounce((query) => {
  fetchResults(query);
}, 300);

// 4. Throttle for scroll/resize events
function throttle(fn, limit) {
  let inThrottle;
  return function(...args) {
    if (!inThrottle) {
      fn.apply(this, args);
      inThrottle = true;
      setTimeout(() => inThrottle = false, limit);
    }
  };
}

window.addEventListener('scroll', throttle(handleScroll, 100));</code></pre>

<h2>Memory Leaks: What to Avoid</h2>
<pre><code>// 1. Remove event listeners when done
const handler = () => console.log("clicked");
button.addEventListener('click', handler);
// Later:
button.removeEventListener('click', handler);

// 2. Clear intervals/timeouts
const interval = setInterval(poll, 1000);
// Later:
clearInterval(interval);

// 3. Avoid global variables
// Bad: data = []; // implicit global
// Good: const data = [];

// 4. Break circular references
let a = {};
let b = { ref: a };
a.ref = b; // Circular!
// Use WeakMap/WeakSet for caches that should not prevent GC
const cache = new WeakMap();
function process(obj) {
  if (cache.has(obj)) return cache.get(obj);
  const result = heavyCalculation(obj);
  cache.set(obj, result);
  return result;
}</code></pre>

<h2>Big-O Notation</h2>
<p>Big-O describes how runtime grows relative to input size. It is the language of algorithm efficiency.</p>
<pre><code>// O(1) — Constant time
function getFirst(arr) { return arr[0]; }

// O(n) — Linear time
function findMax(arr) {
  let max = arr[0];
  for (const num of arr) {
    if (num > max) max = num;
  }
  return max;
}

// O(n²) — Quadratic time (nested loops)
function hasDuplicate(arr) {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    for (let j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
      if (arr[i] === arr[j]) return true;
    }
  }
  return false;
}
// Better: O(n) using a Set!
function hasDuplicateFast(arr) {
  return new Set(arr).size !== arr.length;
}

// O(log n) — Logarithmic (binary search)
// O(n log n) — Efficient sorting (merge sort, quicksort)</code></pre>

<blockquote><strong>Remember:</strong> Premature optimization is the root of all evil. Write clean code first, then profile and optimize the bottlenecks. Use Chrome DevTools Performance tab to find slow functions.</blockquote>"}

{"body":"<h3>1. Inheritance Hierarchies and Method Resolution Order (MRO)</h3>
<p>Inheritance enables child classes to inherit attributes and methods from parent classes, establishing is-a relationships that eliminate boilerplate code across related domains.</p>
<h4>Base Class: BaseAccount</h4>
<h4>SavingsAccount CheckingAccount</h4>
<p>1.1 Class Extensions and super()</p>
<p>The super() builtin returns a proxy object delegating method calls to a parent class. It allows child classes to invoke overridden parent methods or extend initializers without hardcoding base class names.</p>
<pre><code class="py">class BaseAccount:
    def __init__(self, owner: str, balance: float):
        self.owner = owner
        self.balance = balance
    def display_info(self) -&gt; None:
        print(f&quot;Owner: {self.owner} | Balance: ${self.balance:.2f}&quot;)
class SavingsAccount(BaseAccount):
    def __init__(self, owner: str, balance: float, interest_rate: float):
        # Extend parent initialization using super()
        super().__init__(owner, balance)
        self.interest_rate = interest_rate
        1.2 Multiple Inheritance &amp; Method Resolution Order (MRO)</code></pre>
<p>Python supports multiple inheritance, allowing a class to inherit from multiple parent classes. To resolve potential attribute collisions (the &quot;Diamond Problem&quot;), Python uses the C3 Linearization Algorithm to determine its Method Resolution Order (MRO).</p>
<pre><code class="py">class LayerA:
    def execute(self):
        print(&quot;Executing LayerA&quot;)
class LayerB(LayerA):
    def execute(self):
        print(&quot;Executing LayerB&quot;)
        super().execute()
class LayerC(LayerA):
    def execute(self):
        print(&quot;Executing LayerC&quot;)
        # super().execute()
class DiamondChild(LayerB, LayerC):
    def execute(self):
        print(&quot;Executing DiamondChild&quot;)
        super().execute()
        # MRO Inspection
        child = DiamondChild()
        child.execute()
        print(&quot;\n--- MRO Hierarchy ---&quot;)
        for index, cls in enumerate(DiamondChild.__mro__):
            print(f&quot;{index}: {cls.__name__}&quot;)</code></pre>
<h3>2. Abstraction via Abstract Base Classes (ABC)</h3>
<p>Abstraction hides implementation details behind a unified contract. Python enforces abstraction using the abc module and the @abstractmethod decorator.</p>
<pre><code class="py">from abc import ABC, abstractmethod
class BaseTransaction(ABC):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    Abstract Base Class defining the operational blueprint for financial transactions.
    Direct instantiation of BaseTransaction will raise a TypeError.
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    def __init__(self, amount: float):
        self.amount = amount
        super().__init__()
    @abstractmethod
    def validate(self) -&gt; bool:
        &quot;&quot;&quot;Abstract method requiring concrete implementation in subclasses.&quot;&quot;&quot;
        pass
    @abstractmethod
    def execute(self) -&gt; bool:
        &quot;&quot;&quot;Abstract method requiring concrete implementation in subclasses.&quot;&quot;&quot;
        pass</code></pre>
<h4>Attempting to instantiate BaseTransaction directly raises an error</h4>
<pre><code class="py">try:
    tx = BaseTransaction(500.0)
except TypeError as e:
    print(f&quot;Instantiation Blocked: {e}&quot;)
    # Output: Instantiation Blocked: Can&#x27;t instantiate abstract class BaseTransaction with abstract methods execute, validate</code></pre>
<h3>3. Polymorphism &amp; Duck Typing</h3>
<p>Polymorphism allows objects of different types to respond to identical method calls in ways specific to their implementation.</p>
<h3>3.1 Subclass Polymorphism</h3>
<p>Through class hierarchies, subclasses override abstract or base methods, ensuring consistent public interfaces.</p>
<pre><code class="py">class WireTransfer(BaseTransaction):
    def validate(self) -&gt; bool:
        return self.amount &gt; 0 and self.amount &lt;= 100000.0
    def execute(self) -&gt; bool:
        if self.validate():
            print(f&quot;Wire transfer of ${self.amount:.2f}
                executed.&quot;)
            return True
        return False
class CryptoPayment(BaseTransaction):
    def validate(self) -&gt; bool:
        return self.amount &gt; 0.001
    def execute(self) -&gt; bool:
        if self.validate():
            print(f&quot;Crypto blockchain settlement of ${self.amount:.2f} processed.&quot;)
            return True
        return False
    3.2 Duck Typing (&quot;If it walks like a duck...&quot;)</code></pre>
<p>Python favors Duck Typing over rigid static type checking. If an object implements the required methods or behaviors, Python executes them regardless of the object&#x27;s formal inheritance lineage.</p>
<pre><code class="py">class ThirdPartyAdapter:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Class that does NOT inherit from BaseTransaction, but implements duck-compatible interface.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    def execute(self) -&gt; bool:
        print(&quot;Executing payload via Third-Party External System...&quot;)
        return True
    def process_payment(payment_processor) -&gt; None:
        # Works with any object as long as it has an executable .execute() method
        payment_processor.execute()
        # Polymorphic processing across non-homogenous object types
        processors = [WireTransfer(500.0), CryptoPayment(2.5), ThirdPartyAdapter()]
        for p in processors:
            process_payment(p)</code></pre>
<h3>4. Dunder (Magic) Methods &amp; Special Protocol Emulation</h3>
<p>Magic methods (enclosed by double underscores) allow custom classes to hook into Python&#x27;s operator overloading, string formatting, and iteration protocols.</p>
<pre><code class="py">class MonetaryAmount:
    def __init__(self, amount: float, currency: str = &quot;USD&quot;):
        self.amount = float(amount)
        self.currency = currency
        # Developer Representation
    def __repr__(self) -&gt; str:
        return f&quot;MonetaryAmount(amount={self.amount}, currency=&#x27;{self.currency}&#x27;)&quot;
    # User Representation
    def __str__(self) -&gt; str:
        return f&quot;${self.amount:.2f} {self.currency}&quot;
    # Addition Operator Overloading (+)
    def __add__(self, other: &quot;MonetaryAmount&quot;) -&gt; &quot;MonetaryAmount&quot;:
        if not isinstance(other, MonetaryAmount):
            raise TypeError(&quot;Operands must be instances of MonetaryAmount.&quot;)
        if self.currency != other.currency:
            raise ValueError(&quot;Cannot perform arithmetic on differing currencies.&quot;)
        return MonetaryAmount(self.amount + other.amount, self.currency)
    # Equality Comparison (==)
    def __eq__(self, other: object) -&gt; bool:
        if not isinstance(other, MonetaryAmount):
            return False
        return self.amount == other.amount and self.currency == other.currency
    # Greater Than Comparison (&gt;)
    def __gt__(self, other: &quot;MonetaryAmount&quot;) -&gt; bool:
        if self.currency != other.currency:
            raise ValueError(&quot;Currencies must match for comparison.&quot;)
        return self.amount &gt; other.amount
    # Operator Usage Demonstration
    - m1 = MonetaryAmount(150.50)
    m2 = MonetaryAmount(49.50)
    m3 = m1 + m2
    print(&quot;Result of Addition:&quot;, m3) # Output: $200.00 USD
    print(&quot;Equality Check:&quot;, m1 == m2) # Output: False
    print(&quot;Greater Than Check:&quot;, m1 &gt; m2) # Output: True</code></pre>
<h3>5. Composition vs. Inheritance Architecture</h3>
<p>While inheritance establishes is-a relationships, Composition models has-a relationships. Composition embeds self-contained objects within a master object, yielding systems that are more flexible and easier to maintain.</p>
<h4>Composition Architectural Layout</h4>
<pre class="diagram"><code>┌───────────────────────────── ──────────────────┐ │CommercialAccount │ │ ├──owner: str │ │ ├──balance: MonetaryAmount (Object) │ │ └──ledger: AuditLedger (Object) │ └───────────────────────────── ──────────────────┘</code></pre>
<h4>Composition Implementation</h4>
<pre><code class="py">class AuditLedger:
